Given, $P(\overline{A \cup B})=\frac{1}{6}\begin{array}{lr}\Rightarrow & 1-P(A \cup B)=\frac{1}{6} \\ \Rightarrow & 1-P(A)-P(B)+P(A \cap B)=\frac{1}{6}\end{array}
$\begin{array}{lr}\Rightarrow & P(\bar{A})-P(B)+P(A \cap B)=\frac{1}{6} \\ \Rightarrow & \frac{1}{4}-P(B)+\frac{1}{4}=\frac{1}{6}\end{array}
$\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \\ & \text { and } \quad P(B)=\frac{1}{3} \\ & \text { an } \quad P(\bar{A})=1-\frac{1}{4}=\frac{3}{4}\end{aligned}$
Since, $P(A \cap B)=P(A) \cdot P(B)$, so the events $A$ and $B$ are independent events but not equally likely.

Let the probability of success and failure be $p$ and q, respectively
Then,
